WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.830
Hello. My name is John Williams, and I

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.150
have about 30 years experience in

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.110
teaching various electronic engineering

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.550
courses in higher education.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:17.670
And I want to tell you about this course

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:19.630
which I'm making available online

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:22.510
introducing the design of digital

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:25.270
circuits using VHDL.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.590
So, this is a course of videos together

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.590
with a set of practical exercises which

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.190
you can carry out in parallel.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.870
The idea is to introduce the design of

00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.990
synchronous digital circuits using VHDL

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.150
and the FPGA field programmable gate

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:41.430
array

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.350
design flow.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:45.270
It's intended for both undergraduate

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:46.550
students who might want to

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.110
supplement their studies, but also for

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.550
independent learners who already know

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.710
some basic digital electronics, but are

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.430
new to hardware description language

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:57.470
approach.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:00:59.910
The videos will start with the basics of

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:01.950
VHDL, the the hardware description

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:03.670
language we're going to be using,

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:05.509
focusing on synthesis. What I mean by

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:07.790
that is is using it to design hardware

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.870
rather than just simulation. And then

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:11.750
we're also going to cover the design of

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:13.110
synchronous circuits, digital

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:14.790
synchronous circuits.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:17.550
And state machines.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.910
The practical exercises begin with some

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:22.430
simple combinational logic

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.950
just to get you used to using VHDL and

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:26.630
build towards a complete stopwatch

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.630
design with a state machine with a state

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:30.630
machine at the end.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:32.990
So, by the end of the course, you should

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:35.750
be able to read and write

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.870
simple synthesizable VHDL.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.870
You should be able to design and test

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:43.030
combinational and sequential circuits

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.110
using VHDL.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:46.950
You should be able to use a state

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:48.790
machine approach to describe synchronous

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:51.870
behavior in digital circuits.

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:54.870
Write simple test benches. This course

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:56.990
doesn't go very It's not very advanced

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.550
in terms of testing.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:00.190
Um I do provide you with a lot of tests

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.830
rather than get you to write them all,

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:03.430
but you will do some test benching and

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:04.950
and test benches. And also to use

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.510
simulation to check a design.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.710
Uh and you'll be able to synthesize and

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.110
implement a design on a FPGA, a field

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.430
programmable gate array.

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:16.790
Now, prior knowledge, uh I do assume

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.510
that you already have some knowledge of

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:20.910
basic digital logic concepts such as

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.710
different types of gates and Boolean

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.630
logic and or not XOR truth tables and so

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:26.790
on.

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.310
Um so the sort of thing you might cover

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:29.949
in the first year of an electronic

00:02:29.959 --> 00:02:31.630
engineering degree.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:33.550
Um I also I'm going to assume that

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:34.949
you've come across some simple

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:36.870
sequential logic concepts such as

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:39.270
clocks, uh flip-flops, particularly the

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:40.710
D-type flip-flop you might have come

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.990
across, and uh using those as registers.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.470
Um we will be using essentially Visual

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.630
Studio Code as a text editor and

00:02:49.640 --> 00:02:51.190
potentially the terminal as well. So

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:52.670
you'll need some basic knowledge of

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:53.710
that.

00:02:53.720 --> 00:02:56.186
Uh not too much.

00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:59.710
Um you do not need prior experience of

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:02.270
VHDL. So this is this is starting from

00:03:02.280 --> 00:03:04.550
that you know nothing about VHDL as a

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.110
hardware description or any hardware

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:08.509
description language.

00:03:08.519 --> 00:03:11.470
So, strategic goals. Um

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:14.150
well, at the end of this you should be

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.430
confident in designing, testing, and

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:18.949
debugging debugging sequential digital

00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:20.710
circuits to accomplish some useful

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:22.270
goals.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:24.470
You will cover the basics of design

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.350
using a hardware description language

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:28.310
and concepts, and this is more about the

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:30.710
concepts and specific languages central

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.790
to understanding sequential logic design

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:34.470
and state machines.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:36.390
It should therefore prepare you for

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:37.949
subsequent studies if you're doing a

00:03:37.959 --> 00:03:39.670
degree or something like that in digit

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:41.430
more advanced digital system design,

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:43.270
perhaps using more advanced techniques

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:47.150
dealing perhaps uh with unreliable uh

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.949
inputs and things like that. It should

00:03:48.959 --> 00:03:51.110
help you feel cons- confident in

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:53.590
understanding sequential digital logic

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.710
and the state machine approach.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.270
You should know how digital circuits can

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:00.910
be used to tax tackle complex problems.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:02.830
And and hopefully then if you're looking

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.350
for your career, help you position you

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:06.390
for placements, projects, and employment

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.910
in the future.

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:11.230
So, um the first question I suppose for

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:12.510
the first thing I might want to tell us

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:14.150
say is well, why would you want to learn

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:17.349
a hardware description language today?

00:04:17.359 --> 00:04:19.470
So, we'll start with applications.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:20.990
Uh we're using a field programmable

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.590
gate array as a prototyping device. And

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:24.950
of course, you need to use a hardware

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:26.710
description language to program those.

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:28.710
So, if So, applications are FPGA

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:31.150
prototyping and acceleration. FPGAs are

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:33.909
often used to prototype designs before

00:04:33.919 --> 00:04:36.390
you tape them out for a

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:38.710
application-specific circuit. But,

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:40.909
they're also used in some

00:04:40.919 --> 00:04:42.710
as accelerators,

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:45.950
for example, for AI, crypto, and signal

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:47.950
processing in particular.

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:49.390
Um so, you if you're doing those kind of

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.150
applications, you might well need to use

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:54.350
a hardware description language.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.630
A hardware description language isn't

