00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.750
So, I am using VHDL in this course and

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:11.909
so why use VHDL? And

00:07:11.919 --> 00:07:13.909
main main alternative is Verilog. So,

00:07:13.919 --> 00:07:16.790
I've put a note Verilog. So, main

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:19.310
reasons I'm using it are educational.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:21.230
The first thing with uh VHDL is it

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:23.990
enforces hardware awareness uh for you

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.550
as as a when you starting out. So, VHDL

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:28.750
prevents you as a student from treating

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:30.670
the hardware description like a standard

00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:32.510
software programming.

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:34.190
Unfortunately, Verilog is much closer to

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:36.590
a software programming language and it I think

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:39.190
it can be confusing when you start out.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:41.430
So, that's that's the first point. The

00:07:41.440 --> 00:07:44.030
second point is uh VHDL has a very

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:46.430
strongly typed safety.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:48.270
It requires explicit data type

00:07:48.280 --> 00:07:50.550
conversions and that prevents

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:52.950
accidental design errors. And again, I I

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:56.230
consider strongly typed safety an

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:58.550
important especially for beginners.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:00.670
Where when things don't work and without

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:02.630
experience, they they don't always know

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:04.710
where to look and this will catch many

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:06.590
errors before it gets to that stage and

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.350
give you useful useful messages. VHDL is

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:12.950
deterministic has guaranteed behavior

00:08:12.960 --> 00:08:16.110
with strict simulation semantics meaning

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:17.909
that the code behaves identically across

00:08:17.919 --> 00:08:20.190
different software tools.

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:21.909
Again, very useful.

00:08:21.919 --> 00:08:24.750
VHDL forces students to deliberately

00:08:24.760 --> 00:08:26.350
choose between wires, registers, and

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:28.270
buses. So, you have to be explicit and

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:30.190
think about your design. So, explicit

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:33.029
resource allocation. And also, I think

00:08:33.039 --> 00:08:34.630
it has much clearer concurrency

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:36.190
concepts. So, we're designing hardware

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:38.350
here, not software. So, in software, we

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:40.070
think of doing one step at a time.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:41.589
Hardware, we can have lots of things

00:08:41.599 --> 00:08:44.470
going in parallel and the syntax of VHDL

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:46.230
has clearly separates the parallel

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:48.910
hardware blocks from sequential process

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:51.270
loops.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:52.270
So, those are the educational

00:08:52.280 --> 00:08:53.670
advantages. There are some technical

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:55.550
advantages. The strong typing is very

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:57.710
much a technical advantage. It obviously

00:08:57.720 --> 00:08:59.950
stops typing with mismatch errors. It

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:03.110
requires explicit declarations so that

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:05.430
so it prevents those during compilation

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:07.790
rather than hiding them or failing later

00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:10.070
in hardware where they're much much

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:12.816
harder to diagnose and fix.

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:15.630
The deterministic execution is also

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:17.990
something is strictly deterministic.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:19.710
Is also something which I consider a

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:21.630
technical advantage

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:24.030
and it avoids unexpected simulation race

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:25.430
conditions.

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:27.350
These were common in older Verilog. So,

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:29.190
Verilog the language is not does not

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:30.350
guarantee

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:32.430
is not strictly deterministic. Modern

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:33.910
implementations are and it's not a

00:09:33.920 --> 00:09:35.990
problem. But just to be aware that

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:37.910
Verilog as a language does have this

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:40.750
issue. And it is also considered better

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:42.590
for larger projects in terms of how it

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:44.550
hands handles complex packages,

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:46.430
unconstrained records,

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:48.430
and you can use distinct logic libraries

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:50.590
clearly. So, it is considered better for

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:52.390
large projects.

00:09:52.400 --> 00:09:54.390
And in terms of career and industry

00:09:54.400 --> 00:09:55.590
reasons,

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:58.750
defense and aerospace use VHDL a lot in

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:00.870
the UK particularly.

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:02.190
So, it remains a core requirement for

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:05.390
military avionics, space industry, and

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:07.670
government contractor projects.

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:09.950
In terms of regional demand, VHDL is

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:12.430
more commonly used in Europe across

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:14.150
European engineering firms and

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:15.990
universities. Whereas

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:17.910
in Asia and America, I would say Verilog

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:21.630
is is more commonly used.

